1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:02,210
Right. Two really solid sources for this topic.

2
00:00:02,710 --> 00:00:12,650
Yeah, exactly. And our mission today is to decode the exact mechanics of how massive government systems can distribute funds to citizens flawlessly without, you know, reinventing the wheel.

3
00:00:13,530 --> 00:00:18,930
Because, you know, when you travel internationally, you don't rewire your entire hotel room just to charge your laptop, right?

4
00:00:18,970 --> 00:00:21,090
Yeah. I mean, you bring a universal adapter.

5
00:00:21,350 --> 00:00:28,550
Right. Just one small block that connects your specific device to the massive electrical grid that's already, well, sitting right there in the wall.

6
00:00:28,830 --> 00:00:31,870
Which is precisely the philosophy behind the payments block.

7
00:00:32,710 --> 00:00:33,870
Because governments, um,

8
00:00:34,470 --> 00:00:42,010
they frequently fall into the trap of thinking they need to build an entirely new electrical grid from scratch every single time they plug in a new program.

9
00:00:42,230 --> 00:00:44,770
I do want to push back on that premise just a bit, though.

10
00:00:45,190 --> 00:00:48,590
Let's say, um, let's say I'm the Ministry of Education, right?

11
00:00:49,110 --> 00:00:51,270
And I want to issue school grants or scholarships.

12
00:00:51,410 --> 00:00:58,710
Sure. Building my own dedicated payment system sounds like the safest route, I mean, because I control the whole process end to end.

13
00:00:59,290 --> 00:01:01,850
So why isn't that just the standard approach?

14
00:01:02,450 --> 00:01:08,510
Well, it sounds appealing in theory, but in practice, that creates completely siloed, duplicated systems.

15
00:01:09,210 --> 00:01:16,210
I mean, if every ministry built its own payout infrastructure, the government ends up maintaining dozens of redundant, overlapping networks.

16
00:01:16,530 --> 00:01:19,490
And I imagine those are incredibly costly to change later on.

17
00:01:19,850 --> 00:01:20,730
Oh, absolutely.

18
00:01:21,550 --> 00:01:24,990
The GovStack payments block is definitely not a new payment scheme.

19
00:01:25,590 --> 00:01:35,470
It simply acts as one shared connection sitting directly between the government's account systems, like a central bank, for instance, and the existing public or private payment networks that are

20
00:01:35,530 --> 00:01:36,870
already operating in the market.

21
00:01:37,430 --> 00:01:39,950
Okay, but I'm still a bit stuck on the actual mechanics here.

22
00:01:40,430 --> 00:01:48,690
If my ministry plugs into this block instead of cutting checks directly, what is this block actually doing actively behind the scenes?

23
00:01:49,370 --> 00:01:53,150
Well, we can look at one critical mechanism inside, which is the account mapper.

24
00:01:54,090 --> 00:01:56,390
When your ministry authorizes a payment to a citizen,

25
00:01:57,050 --> 00:02:01,550
you might just have their national ID number or, uh, maybe a program registration number.

26
00:02:02,110 --> 00:02:05,670
Right. So you don't necessarily have their actual bank routing number.

27
00:02:06,150 --> 00:02:10,790
Exactly. So the account mapper acts like a giant automated switchboard.

28
00:02:11,190 --> 00:02:16,250
It instantly translates that ID into the citizen's specific current bank routing details.

29
00:02:17,070 --> 00:02:22,430
Oh, wow. So without the ministry having to store or constantly update sensitive banking information themselves.

30
00:02:22,870 --> 00:02:23,250
Spot on.

31
00:02:23,970 --> 00:02:29,150
And then the block uses its shared payment gateway to route that translated request outward.

32
00:02:30,110 --> 00:02:37,230
Okay, but pushing all those funds through a central switchboard brings up a huge concern for me, which is security and compliance.

33
00:02:37,710 --> 00:02:39,050
Yeah, that's a very common concern.

34
00:02:39,390 --> 00:02:42,490
Right. Because if the anti-money laundering checks are pushed out to the banks,

35
00:02:43,110 --> 00:02:48,750
does that create a massive blind spot for the government, or does it, um, actually reduce their liability?

36
00:02:49,410 --> 00:02:56,290
It strategically reduces their liability, actually, by leveraging the safeguards that are already legally established out in the market.

37
00:02:57,190 --> 00:03:02,630
That boundary line is crucial here. All verification stays strictly outside the block.

38
00:03:03,150 --> 00:03:05,330
Okay. So the identification of people and all that.

39
00:03:06,070 --> 00:03:13,590
Yeah, the identification, the settlement of funds between institutions, and all the strict Know Your Customer and anti-money laundering checks,

40
00:03:14,090 --> 00:03:16,570
all of that stays with the financial institutions.

41
00:03:17,430 --> 00:03:18,190
That makes perfect sense.

42
00:03:19,110 --> 00:03:23,330
It's essentially the difference between the post office and the police, right?

43
00:03:23,790 --> 00:03:25,130
Oh, I like that analogy. Yeah.

44
00:03:25,790 --> 00:03:31,990
Because the post office, which is the payments block in this case, is just designed to route the package to the right address efficiently.

45
00:03:32,690 --> 00:03:38,870
The police, meaning the banking regulators, handle the security and legality of what's actually moving through the system.

46
00:03:39,490 --> 00:03:44,010
Right. And separating those duties is exactly what allows the system to move so fast.

47
00:03:44,850 --> 00:03:50,990
So if the government stays out of the banking side, what does this actually look like when the money leaves the treasury?

48
00:03:51,210 --> 00:03:53,910
I mean, let's trace a real payment path from start to finish.

49
00:03:54,830 --> 00:04:01,010
Sure. We can trace the flow in Progresa, which is, uh, the fictional demonstration country used in the reference architecture.

50
00:04:01,690 --> 00:04:03,530
The path is actually highly linear.

51
00:04:04,430 --> 00:04:05,309
Okay. Where does it start?

52
00:04:06,110 --> 00:04:08,210
It starts at the government programs account.

53
00:04:08,690 --> 00:04:15,650
The payments block receives a batch of payout requests, and then that account mapper finds the specific destination for each beneficiary.

54
00:04:16,430 --> 00:04:16,930
Okay, got it.

55
00:04:17,910 --> 00:04:24,130
Then the block hands that translated batch to the payer bank, which is the specific bank holding the government's funds.

56
00:04:24,630 --> 00:04:30,550
Meaning the government's bank receives the batch and just executes the payment through whatever market payment systems already exist, like,

57
00:04:31,390 --> 00:04:35,510
say, a local fictional provider called PayPro, routing it straight to the citizen's account.

58
00:04:35,690 --> 00:04:40,470
Exactly. But does mandating the shared connection model actually work at scale in the real world,

59
00:04:41,090 --> 00:04:42,670
or does it just become a huge bottleneck?

60
00:04:42,970 --> 00:04:50,650
It definitely works at scale. The 2022 Bank for International Settlements bulletin provides incredible real-world proof of this

61
00:04:50,950 --> 00:04:53,110
with Brazil's instant payment system, Pix.

62
00:04:53,790 --> 00:04:55,110
Oh, right. Pix is huge down there.

63
00:04:55,290 --> 00:04:57,670
Yeah. And the central bank didn't build a closed loop.

64
00:04:58,270 --> 00:05:02,170
They operated the connective tissue and required the large banks to participate.

65
00:05:02,930 --> 00:05:10,190
So because Pix connected existing financial institutions rather than, you know, attempting to bypass or fragment them, it achieved

66
00:05:10,530 --> 00:05:11,430
staggering adoption?

67
00:05:12,230 --> 00:05:17,850
Staggering is right. Sixty-seven percent of Brazilian adults used it within 15 months of its launch.

68
00:05:18,630 --> 00:05:20,070
Wait, really? Yeah.

69
00:05:20,310 --> 00:05:22,090
Sixty-seven percent in 15 months?

70
00:05:22,250 --> 00:05:27,010
Yeah. The mechanism of their success was leveraging the infrastructure the market already did well.

71
00:05:27,450 --> 00:05:32,210
So the core takeaway for you today is that the payments block isn't a new payment system.

72
00:05:32,650 --> 00:05:38,450
It's a vital standardized connection ensuring every government program pays through one shared infrastructure.

73
00:05:39,330 --> 00:05:47,710
Exactly. It eliminates duplication and allows government ministries to focus entirely on their actual missions rather than managing financial rails.

74
00:05:48,370 --> 00:05:50,970
Which brings us right back to that international travel adapter.

